{"author":{"id":"585844baa6074d44d241f46aa7c9f21ff46653adf617d7f070fbfe327626c989"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794517767,"nonce":"KugwkDYMJYg6FIoR7YdPH+bdueI=","message":"Closing: hxn import is done as scoped. Final commit ec308a0.\n\nec308a0 adds arrays of numbers: a const pointer to a fixed-width integer, float or double with a count after it is sequence\u003cT\u003e (a slice in Rust, an Array in Haxe); a non-const one is an array C fills, [HxnOut] sequence\u003cT\u003e, which the caller sizes and gets back, when the C function returns nothing or a status. Writable bytes and arrays now need the count to be named as one (n, len, size, count, *_len, n_*, ...). That fixes a false positive in 0.2.0: sqlite3_deserialize's buffer, which sqlite keeps, was imported as [HxnWritten] bytes; it is now left out, while sqlite3_blob_read stays. The generated objects' raw pointer and the ffi module are pub(crate), so a library writes what the WebIDL cannot describe in its own #[hxn::methods] block beside the bindings; tests/cimport/extra.rs does that.\n\nWhat hxn import covers: opaque objects and their free functions, enums, structs by value and by pointer, text in and out (and text the caller frees), bytes and arrays in and out, outputs through a pointer with status codes, borrowed objects, callbacks lent for a call or kept as streams, destructors of text and bytes, constants passed for a pointer, functions the linked library lacks left out, and system libraries linked into hxcpp programs.\n\nLeft out by design, recorded in docs/design.md: C++ (its author provides a C API), objects passed to callbacks (C owns them past the call), callbacks whose user data is in the middle or that take other untyped pointers, variadic functions and va_list, and pointers whose meaning the header does not give.\n\nRun on ec308a0: tests/cimport/run.sh on Ash (hybrid, jit, interp), hxcpp, CPPIA and Emscripten, with sqlite3 imported unedited where it runs. Not run yet: CI (Linux, Windows).","files":null}]}